In a communitarian outlook, identity and moral worth arise from belonging. Obligations radiate outward: first one’s household, then the village, then those who knock seeking entry. Refusing all aid would fracture the village’s moral self-understanding; yet empty-handed largesse that endangers current members would likewise dissolve the very community meant to help. The constructive path is to widen the circle—accept the newcomers as provisional members—while instituting fair, collectively agreed limits so both groups survive and can eventually thrive together. Hence prudently-structured sharing carries the greatest weight; absolute refusal undervalues solidarity, and unconditional aid risks the whole. 

For the underlying principles, safeguarding communal identity (we do not let neighbors perish) and sober assessment of risk (we must endure to keep the bond alive) are both critical; strict kin-first partiality matters, but less decisively once others stand at the gate asking to join the circle.

